diff --git a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
index 15a57c6..d1b12cc 100644
--- a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -283,0 +284,10 @@ Upstream restore-state persistence remains upstream-owned. The wrapper passes an
+## Tron-owned host
+
+Tron supplies `ownedProfilePath` and the pinned engine executable when registering this package through Pi's built-in extension loader. The native integration remains the only launch, restore, daemon and CDP owner; the host does not launch or broker browsers. Other package hosts do not acquire Tron's ownership authority.
+
+Owned mode uses one persistent custom profile and a private native HOME/socket pool. A profile setting is not an attachment receipt. After local execution, the native owner records the exact loopback endpoint, listener PID, process-start identity, issued launch nonce and profile command line. Every attachment revalidates that record before invoking the native CLI. Existing native execution claims serialize the store across runtimes; atomic records and bounded sessions survive reloads. The owned browser is always launched by the profile-bound root session (`tron-<profile hash>`) with native restore keyed to that session; any other session, including wrapper-managed and `fresh` sessions, first triggers that root launch and then attaches over CDP, so wrapper-managed sessions keep their restore-disabled policy. Restore state is saved on close and periodically under the private owned HOME (`.agent-browser/sessions/`), whose parents are mode 0700, and is restored by the next root launch after a Gateway or browser restart. Session cookies therefore outlive browser close: a login held only in a session cookie survives restarts, independent of Chrome's own session-cookie handling.
+
+Generic `connect` remains available for recorded launches. Native Electron launch records its child before handoff, including known page endpoints. An external owned Chrome producer such as studio uses `owned-launch prepare`, launches ordinary Chrome with the returned arguments, then calls `owned-launch record <launchId> <pid>` before connecting to the returned endpoint. These are launch receipts, not a second launcher. After that temporary child has exited, `owned-launch cleanup <launchId>` retires its profile. Unknown or unrecorded cleanup ownership is refused and retained for diagnosis; no PID-file-based signalling is added.
+
+Personal profile selection/copying, auto-discovered or unrecorded endpoints, imported browser state, opaque plugin/flow runners and system-browser-opening commands are refused. Attachments must be standalone so their receipt precedes page work; batches may operate on an already attached owned browser. Existing personal-Chrome login state is unavailable and is never copied. New logins in the owned profile persist through native close/restart.
+
